# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Method sources and run artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The methods HarnessGrad adapts are OTHER PEOPLE'S repositories. They are not
# vendored: adapters/SOURCES.md records what was read and adapters/fetch.sh
# retrieves it. They live as a sibling directory of this repo, so a clone of
# this repo contains code and records -- not datasets, papers or their licenses.
*_runs/

# Run artifacts: every run contains a workspace that is itself a git repo, so
# committing runs/ would nest repositories and grow without bound.
runs/

# Runs archived rather than deleted, when a batch was declared meaningless. Same
# reason as runs/, and named separately so an archive is never mistaken for a live
# result a reader could compare against.
runs_discarded_*/

# Non-code assets that arrive attached to method repositories. Kept out so a
# clone of this repo contains code and records, not datasets and papers.
# NOTE: lock files are deliberately NOT ignored -- they belong in the repo,
# they are what makes an environment reproducible.
*.pdf
**/docs/assets/
**/predictions/
**/logs/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.env` holds real keys for the model behind the harness. It is written by hand
# and never committed; `.env.example` is the committed template that says which
# variables a run needs. This rule is added before the file exists, because a
# secret that has been committed once is compromised even after it is deleted.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
# `.env.example` is deliberately NOT ignored: it is the committed template that
# says which variables a run needs. A bare `.env.*` pattern would swallow it, and
# the negation below would not rescue it -- git does not re-include a file whose
# own pattern matched. Enumerating the concrete names is the reliable form.

# Exported harnesses: the finished artifacts of runs, browsable rather than buried
# in a run's git. They are copies -- the commits they came from stay in runs/ --
# so regenerating them is `python tools/export_harnesses.py`.
current_harness/

# Harness working trees. Outside the platform tree on purpose: a harness placed
# inside it can reach the driver and .env through `..`.
harnessgrad_work/

# 服务 PID 文件:本地运行状态,不该进版本库
.harnessgrad-state/
